hing using Gemini Argon. ...      * Riemann Hypothesis (`riemann_104`, `riemann_120`, and `riemann_744`): All three machines (`riemann_104`, Shunia's 120-state machine `riemann_120`, and the 744-state machine `riemann_744`) halt if and only if Mathlib's `RiemannHypothesis` is false (`Halts candidate ↔ ¬ RiemannHypothesis`, `Halts riemann120 ↔ ¬ RiemannHypothesis`, and `Halts riemann744 ↔ ¬ RiemannHypothesis`).\n\n# Certified State Reductions and Semantics of Conjecture-Checking Turing Machines\n\nThis repository contains formally verified 2-symbol Turing machines and Lean 4 semantics proofs for Goldbach's Conjecture, ZF Set Theory Consistency, and the Riemann Hypothesis, developed by Maxence Faldor and Moritz Firsching using Gemini Argon.\n\n## Formalized Machines\n\nConjecture  | Machine  | States  | Source / Provenance  | Proved Halting Condition (`Halts M ↔ ...`)  | Lean 4 Proofs\n--- | --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nGoldbach's Conjecture  | `goldbach_21`  | `21`  | Reduced from `goldbach_25` (`25` states, Yao / Leng 2024)  | `¬ GoldbachConjecture`  | `Goldbach21.lean`, `Semantics/Goldbach.lean`\nZF Consistency  | `zf_380`  | `380`  | Reduced from `zf_393` (`393` states, Wade Aug 2026)  | `ZFInconsistent` (`¬ Halts candidate` proved)  | `Zf380.lean`, `Semantics/Zf393.lean`\nZF Consistency  | `zf_393`  | `393`  | Andrew Wade (`turing_machine_explorer` `9c5c2e82`, Aug 2026)  | `ZFInconsistent` (`¬ Halts zf393` proved)  | `Zf393.lean`, `Semantics/Zf393.lean`\nZF Consistency  | `zf_432`  | `432`  | Andrew Wade (`turing_machine_explorer` `33b30300`, Aug 2025)  | `WadeZFInconsistent`  | `Semantics/Zf.lean`\nRiemann Hypothesis  | `riemann_104`  | `104`  | Reduced from `riemann_120` (`120` states, Shunia Sept 2026)  | `¬ RiemannHypothesis`  | `Riemann104.lean`, `Semantics/Riemann120.lean`\nRiemann Hypothesis  | `riemann_120`  | `120`  | Joseph M. Shunia (`arXiv:2609.30306`, Sept 2026)  | `¬ RiemannHypothesis`  | `Riemann120.lean`, `Semantics/Riemann120.lean`\nRiemann Hypothesis  | `riemann_744`  | `744`  | Matiyasevich, O'Rear & Aaronson (`riemann_744_sorear.tm`, 2016)  | `¬ RiemannHypothesis`  | `Semantics/Riemann.lean`\n\nSee `RELATED_WORK.md` for the historical progression across each domain.\n\n## What Is Formally Proved\n\nAll proofs in `Cryptids/` are checked by the Lean 4 kernel (`v4.35.0-rc4`) without `sorry` and use only the standard axioms (`propext`, `Classical.choice`, `Quot.sound`):\n\n  1. State reduction and halting equivalence (`candidate.states < reference.states` and `Halts candidate ↔ Halts reference`): Each reduced record machine (`goldbach_21`, `zf_380`, `riemann_104`) has fewer states than its reference machine (`goldbach_25`, `zf_393`, `riemann_120`) and halts from the blank tape if and only if the reference machine halts (`Goldbach21.lean`, `Zf380.lean`, `Riemann104.lean`).\n  2. End-to-end mathematical semantics (`Cryptids/Semantics/`):\n     * Goldbach (`goldbach_21` and `goldbach_25`): Both `goldbach_21` and `goldbach_25` halt if and only if Goldbach's conjecture is false (`Halts candidate ↔ ¬ GoldbachConjecture`).\n     * Riemann Hypothesis (`riemann_104`, `riemann_120`, and `riemann_744`): All three machines (`riemann_104`, Shunia's 120-state machine `riemann_120`, and the 744-state machine `riemann_744`) halt if and only if Mathlib's `RiemannHypothesis` is false (`Halts candidate ↔ ¬ RiemannHypothesis`, `Halts riemann120 ↔ ¬ RiemannHypothesis`, and `Halts riemann744 ↔ ¬ RiemannHypothesis`).\n     * ZF Consistency (`zf_380`, `zf_393`, and `zf_432`): Both `zf_380` and Wade's 393-state machine `zf_393` (incorporating upstream fix `10fcf152b3`) halt if and only if O'Rear's `zf2.nql` Hilbert system proves $v_0 \\in v_0$ (`Halts candidate ↔ ZFInconsistent`, `Halts zf393 ↔ ZFInconsistent`), and hence never halt in `ZFSet.{0}` (`not_halts_candidate`, `not_halts_zf393`), while `zf_432` halts if and only if the 20-clause deductive system enumerated by `zf_432_wade.tm` derives $v_0 \\in v_0$ (`WadeZFInconsistent`).\n\nDetails on the proof architecture and mathematical background are in `Cryptids/Semantics/README.md`.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe additive indecomposability of the primes (https://github.com/openai/math/tree/main/preprints/the-additive-indecomposability-of-the-primes-September-24-2026)\nciteturn2academia12 [wordlim: 200] Published: last month; Authors: OpenAI ... We prove Ostmann's inverse Goldbach conjecture: no set differing from the primes by finitely many elements can be written as $A+B$, where A and B are sets of nonnegative integers with at least two elements each.\nTitle: The additive indecomposability of the primes\nAuthors: OpenAI\nDate: Thu Sep 24 00:00:00 2026\n\nWe prove Ostmann's inverse Goldbach conjecture: no set differing from the primes by finitely many elements can be written as $A+B$, where A and B are sets of nonnegative integers with at least two elements each.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nmath/README.md at main · openai/math · GitHub (https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/README.md?plain=1)\nciteturn2search2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 days ago; This repository contains mathematical manuscripts and supporting proof artifacts produced by an unnamed internal OpenAI model. ... We defer to the math community completely on matters of credit, precedence, and concurrent work. ... A family groups related papers, which may include a principal result, companion arguments, consequences, or alternative proofs. ... - The [Lean library](lean/README.md) and [formalization catalogue](lean/formalization.yaml) describe the available formal proofs, their associated papers, and verification configurations. ... Exceptions to this fixed procedure include work on a zero-free region for the Riemann zeta function and proof of the Hodge Conjecture for CM abelian varieties.\n\n# Readme\n\nThis repository contains mathematical manuscripts and supporting proof artifacts produced by an unnamed internal OpenAI model.\n\nAs part of model development, we evaluate our models on open research problems. We expanded these evaluations after performance on our existing mathematical evaluations saturated. Some outputs build upon earlier results produced by the models.\n\nThis collection includes results at different stages of verification. Not all have accompanying Lean formalizations. We will continue to update this repository with Lean formalizations as we obtain them.\n\nSome of the unformalized results could have issues. We will endeavor to fix any such issues quickly.\n\nWe are also exploring community-hosted repositories for these materials.\n\nIn our view, these manuscripts are best understood as a form of raw research data, rather than polished academic papers. We defer to the math community completely on matters of credit, precedence, and concurrent work.\n\n## Navigating the collection\n\nThe current catalogue contains 721 manuscripts organized into 373 families. A family groups related papers, which may include a principal result, companion arguments, consequences, or alternative proofs. Each family is classified by mathematical discipline.\n\n- Start with the [overview](overview.pdf) for descriptions of the families.\n\n- Use the [manuscript map](CONTENTS.md) to find individual papers and their supporting materials.\n\n- The [`preprints/`](preprints/) directory contains PDFs, source files, and manuscript-specific citation and build instructions.\n\n- The [Lean library](lean/README.md) and [formalization catalogue](lean/formalization.yaml) describe the available formal proofs, their associated papers, and verification configurations. See the [Comparator instructions](lean/ComparatorChallenges/README.md) for additional checking instructions. The repository has ~42% top-line results formalized.\n\n- Updates to the repo are described in the [history](history.md).\n\n### Reasoning summaries\n\nWe are also releasing abridged summaries of the model's reasoning, covering the following results:\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\ncryptids/Cryptids/Semantics/README.md at main · google-deepmind/cryptids · GitHub (https://github.com/google-deepmind/cryptids/blob/main/Cryptids/Semantics/README.md)\nciteturn2search3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 weeks ago; This directory contains formal Lean 4 proofs connecting the Turing machines in `Cryptids/` to the mathematical conjectures they encode, along with full provenance and attribution for the underlying constructions. ... In `Cryptids/Semantics/Goldbach.lean`, we define Goldbach's conjecture in standard mathematical terms on top of Mathlib's `Nat.Prime`: ...      * Author: Yijun Leng (`github.com/lengyijun/goldbach_tm`, Apache-2.0 License).\n\n# Formal Semantics of Conjecture Turing Machines (`Cryptids/Semantics/`)\n\nThis directory contains formal Lean 4 proofs connecting the Turing machines in `Cryptids/` to the mathematical conjectures they encode, along with full provenance and attribution for the underlying constructions.\n\n## 1. Goldbach's Conjecture (`Goldbach.lean`)\n\n### Main Theorems\n\nIn `Cryptids/Semantics/Goldbach.lean`, we define Goldbach's conjecture in standard mathematical terms on top of Mathlib's `Nat.Prime`:\n    \n    def GoldbachConjecture : Prop :=\n      ∀ n : ℕ, 2 < n → Even n → ∃ p q : ℕ, Nat.Prime p ∧ Nat.Prime q ∧ n = p + q\n\nand prove that both the 25-state baseline machine `reference` (`goldbach_25`) and the 21-state compressed machine `candidate` (`goldbach_21`) halt on the blank tape if and only if Goldbach's conjecture is false:\n    \n    theorem halts_reference_iff_not_goldbach :\n        Halts reference ↔ ¬ GoldbachConjecture\n    \n    theorem halts_candidate_iff_not_goldbach :\n        Halts candidate ↔ ¬ GoldbachConjecture\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGitHub - aster2024/openai-math-followups: Follow-ups to the OpenAI mathematics release (October 2026): papers, sources and Lean ancillary files · GitHub (https://github.com/aster2024/openai-math-followups)\nciteturn2search4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 days ago; ### `polynomial-goldbach-exceptions/` — Exceptional sets for sums and differences of two primes along polynomial sequences ... For a fixed integer polynomial Q of degree d = 2 or 3 with positive leading coefficient, the number of m ≤ N for which Q(m) is positive and even but not a sum of two primes is ... relative to the fourth-moment and major-arc theorems of Li–Liu (arXiv:2610.26756) under a common fixed Dirichlet zero-free boundary Θ < 11/12, which OpenAI's 7/8 theorem supplies; the same holds for differences of two primes of size O(N^d). ... $$\\#\\{\\,n:\\ N/3\\le n\\le 2N/3,\\ \\ P^-(n)\\gt z,\\ \\ P^-(N-n)\\gt z,\\ \\ \\lambda(n)=\\sigma,\\ \\ \\lambda(N-n)=\\tau\\,\\}\\ \\ge\\ c\\,\\frac{N}{(\\log N)^{1/4}}, \\qquad z=\\exp\\bigl((\\log N)^{1/10}\\bigr),$$\nThe method is the classical thin-set exceptional-set mechanism. Quadratics mapping an interval into [X/2, X] have O(X^(1/4+ε)) exceptions uniformly in the coefficients.\n\n### `rough-goldbach-parity/` — Sums of two rough numbers with prescribed Liouville signs\n\nPaper (24 pages), 9 October 2026; attributions updated 2 November 2026: PDF. Programs checking finite instances of the algebraic identities are in `rough-goldbach-parity/anc/`.\n\nLet λ be the Liouville function and P⁻(n) the least prime factor of n. For every sufficiently large even integer N and each pair of signs (σ, τ),\n\n$$\\#\\{\\,n:\\ N/3\\le n\\le 2N/3,\\ \\ P^-(n)\\gt z,\\ \\ P^-(N-n)\\gt z,\\ \\ \\lambda(n)=\\sigma,\\ \\ \\lambda(N-n)=\\tau\\,\\}\\ \\ge\\ c\\,\\frac{N}{(\\log N)^{1/4}}, \\qquad z=\\exp\\bigl((\\log N)^{1/10}\\bigr),$$\n\nand n, N − n may also be taken squarefree with at most ⌈2 log log N⌉ prime factors.\n\nThe proof combines the zero-free half-plane Re s > 7/8 for Dirichlet L-functions with the weighted dilation graphs of OpenAI's paper Weighted dilation graphs, smooth shifted primes and totient fibers (24 September 2026). Two of the graph estimates are needed for displacements that are multiples of N and for prime groups with the prime factors of N removed; the paper obtains these from the proofs given there, going through every place where the displacement and the interval structure of the groups enter.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAn unconditional first moment for cubic Gauss sums (https://github.com/openai/math/tree/main/preprints/An-unconditional-first-moment-for-cubic-Gauss-sums-September-25-2026)\nciteturn2academia13 [wordlim: 200] Published: last month; Authors: OpenAI ... We prove Patterson's first-moment conjecture for normalized cubic Gauss sums over all primary Eisenstein primes, unconditionally.\nTitle: An unconditional first moment for cubic Gauss sums\nAuthors: OpenAI\nDate: Fri Sep 25 00:00:00 2026\n\nWe prove Patterson's first-moment conjecture for normalized cubic Gauss sums over all primary Eisenstein primes, unconditionally. The sharp-cutoff main term is $(6/5)c_*X^{5/6}/\\log X$, where $c_*=(2\\pi)^{2/3}/(3\\Gamma(2/3))$. For every fixed nonzero angular Fourier mode of the prime argument, we also prove cancellation at the first-moment scale.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nE-320: Teaching Math with a Historical Perspective (https://people.math.harvard.edu/~knill/teaching/mathe320_2014/handouts/03-numbertheory.pdf)\nciteturn2search14 [wordlim: 200] Published: last year; Goldbach ... Given a prime like 7 and a number n we can add or subtract multiples of 7 from n to get a number ... = −1 modulo n.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTwo Theorems To Verify Goldbach's Strong Conjecture ; or To Refute it By an Uninterrupted Sequence of Composite Odd Numbers In The Interval [n ─ 2n] (https://www.opastpublishers.com/open-access-articles/two-theorems-to-verify-goldbachs-strong-conjecture-or-to-refute-it-by-an-uninterrupted-sequence-of-composite-odd-numbers-in-the-in-9089.html)\nciteturn2search5 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.6 years ago; Crawled: last month; By contrast, if only one X is prime, then GSC is true. ... As long as we cannot find this very precious and historical counterexample of an uninterrupted sequence of composite numbers by E – P2 = X (P2 is in E/2-E interval); Goldbach's strong conjecture will remain true although unprovable. This article gives the TWO THEOREMS of CONGRUENCE-MODULO which always predetermine whether GSC is true or not when calculating E - P2 = X (see above).\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGoldfeld's analytic density conjecture and the 2-converse for elliptic curves (https://github.com/openai/math/tree/main/preprints/Goldfelds-analytic-density-conjecture-and-the-2-converse-for-elliptic-curves-October-7-2026)\nciteturn2academia15 [wordlim: 200] Published: last month; Authors: OpenAI ... We prove Goldfeld's analytic density conjecture: for every elliptic curve E over ℚ, the quadratic twists of E with analytic rank zero and one each have density 1/2 among signed squarefree twist parameters ordered by absolute value. We also prove the low-corank 2-converse: if the $2^\\infty$-Selmer corank of E is zero or one, then it equals the analytic and Mordell–Weil ranks, and the Tate–Shafarevich group is finite.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nlean-pool/AGENTS.md at main · LeanPool/lean-pool · GitHub (https://github.com/LeanPool/lean-pool/blob/main/AGENTS.md)\nciteturn2search6 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 5 days ago; Tau Ceti is also a pinned Lake dependency; import its modules directly instead of vendoring projects already in Tau Ceti or Mathlib. See `MOTIVATION.md` for the why, browse the API docs at https://leanpool.github.io/lean-pool/,